# https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Core/ExternalIdentifierType/ibid node809
node809 [ shape=none, color=black label=< <table color='#666666' cellborder='0' cellspacing='0' border='1'><tr><td colspan='2' bgcolor='grey'><B>ibid</B></td></tr><tr><td href='https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Core/ExternalIdentifierType/ibid' bgcolor='#eeeeee' colspan='2'><font point-size='10' color='#6666ff'>https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Core/ExternalIdentifierType/ibid</font></td></tr><tr><td align='left'>rdfs:comment</td><td align='left'>&quot;[International Business Identifier (IBID)](https://ekys.org/business_identifiers/) is a verifiable unique identifier for legal entities as defined in the [ISO/DIS 25500-3](https://www.iso.org/standard/87705.html) draft standard. It is composed of three data elements: the International Business Registration Number (IBRN), the legal name, and the date of origin formatted as eight numeric characters (YYYYMMDD). Each element is separated by a pipe character (`|`) (e.g., `US-DE.BER:3031657|Code Management Association|19990420`).&quot;@en</td></tr></table> > ] 
# https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Core/RelationshipType/hasDataFile node810
node810 [ shape=none, color=black label=< <table color='#666666' cellborder='0' cellspacing='0' border='1'><tr><td colspan='2' bgcolor='grey'><B>hasDataFile</B></td></tr><tr><td href='https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Core/RelationshipType/hasDataFile' bgcolor='#eeeeee' colspan='2'><font point-size='10' color='#6666ff'>https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Core/RelationshipType/hasDataFile</font></td></tr><tr><td align='left'>rdfs:comment</td><td align='left'>&quot;The `from` Element treats each `to` Element as a data file. A data file is an artifact that stores data required or optional for the `from` Element&#x27;s functionality. A data file can be a database file, an index file, a log file, an AI model file, a calibration data file, a temporary file, a backup file, and more. For AI training dataset, test dataset, test artifact, configuration data, build input data, and build output data, please consider using the more specific relationship types: `trainedOn`, `testedOn`, `hasTest`, `configures`, `hasInput`, and `hasOutput`, respectively. This relationship does not imply dependency.&quot;@en</td></tr></table> > ] 
